/**
 * @defgroup DspCore_Control Control
 * @brief Signal and system control with explicit timing, state and limits.
 *
 * Include `<DspCore_Control.h>` or a component header under `<DspCore/Control/>`.
 * Smoother, LinearRamp, SlewLimiter, Hysteresis, PID and PidParameters are
 * trivially copyable float/double value types with compile-time size budgets.
 * Map and Deadband are stateless functions. No component allocates, throws,
 * schedules callbacks, reads a clock or interprets MIDI messages.
 *
 * @section DspCore_Control_Contracts Timing and state contracts
 *
 * - **SPEC-CTL-2**: dt and durations use seconds. Updates require a finite,
 *   strictly positive dt; configuration durations/time constants may be zero
 *   where explicitly documented. Caller supplies the actual interval. No
 *   sample-rate, wall-clock or fixed-period assumption is hidden in a class.
 * - **SPEC-CTL-3**: Every numeric parameter/input must be finite and satisfy
 *   its documented domain. Bool failure leaves configuration, dynamic state
 *   and output unchanged. Unrepresentable intermediate arithmetic also fails.
 *   Callers must handle failure; a held old output is not a new measurement.
 *   Reset is explicit; valid Configure preserves state except documented
 *   clamping when PID limits change. Copying an object copies its whole state.
 * - **SPEC-CTL-4**: Mapping boundary behavior and output limits are explicit.
 *   Reversed output mapping is supported; reversed input bounds are rejected.
 *   Deadband is continuous and subtracts its width outside the zero region.
 *   Hysteresis holds state between inclusive lower and upper thresholds.
 * - **SPEC-CTL-5**: PID has derivative on measurement, output saturation and
 *   conditional integration. Integral and output limits share the configured
 *   output range. The first measurement after Reset produces no derivative
 *   impulse. Reset/configuration and derivative filtering follow the PID topic.
 * - **SPEC-CTL-6**: Updates/configuration/reset allocate no memory, use only
 *   caller-owned state, and never enable global denormal flushing. Public
 *   scalar types stay float/double across targets; AVR double may equal float.
 *   Denormal/rounding resolution follows the target. There are no hardware
 *   throughput, control-stability or actuator-specific guarantees.
 *
 * Components use generic signal units: amplitudes, volts, positions or other
 * caller-defined values. Gains, rates and thresholds must use compatible units.
 * MIDILAR may bind MIDI controllers to these components; that interpretation
 * remains outside DspCore. See @ref DspCore_Numeric.
 */
/**
 * @defgroup DspCore_Control_Smoothing Smoothing, ramps and slew
 * @ingroup DspCore_Control
 * @brief Explicit time constants, target durations and asymmetric rate limits.
 *
 * Smoother implements y=lerp(y,input,1-exp(-dt/tau)). Default tau=0 bypasses
 * smoothing. Configure(tau) preserves Value; Reset(value) sets it explicitly.
 * Tiny dt/tau uses a stable series rather than cancellation-prone subtraction;
 * ratios >=40 select the destination, indistinguishable at supported precision.
 *
 * LinearRamp starts at zero and idle. SetTarget(target,seconds) starts from
 * the current value; retargeting preserves continuity. A zero duration snaps
 * immediately. Update consumes seconds and reaches Target exactly when the
 * supplied interval meets/exceeds the remaining duration. Reset cancels the
 * ramp. Sub-resolution dt may not advance represented elapsed time.
 *
 * SlewLimiter defaults to rise=fall=1 value-unit/second. Configure takes
 * nonnegative rise/fall rates; zero holds that direction. Reset sets current
 * value. Update limits the step and snaps exactly to an input within reach.
 * A nonrepresentable delta or rate*dt returns failure rather than saturating
 * an intermediate silently. These components do not generate MIDI timing.
 * Examples: Control/Smoothing and Control/RampSlew.
 */
/**
 * @defgroup DspCore_Control_Mapping Mapping, deadband and hysteresis
 * @ingroup DspCore_Control
 * @brief Explicit range mapping and noise-resistant control decisions.
 *
 * Map(input,inputLow,inputHigh,outputLow,outputHigh,output,boundary) requires
 * ascending finite input bounds with representable span. Output direction may
 * be increasing, decreasing or constant. Clamp limits the input to its domain;
 * Reject fails outside it; Extrapolate permits outside values. All results
 * must be finite. MappingBoundary is a one-byte enum.
 *
 * Deadband maps input in [-width,width] to zero, input>width to input-width,
 * and input<-width to input+width. Width must be nonnegative. It does not
 * rescale the remaining range or introduce a hidden center offset.
 *
 * Hysteresis starts inactive with lower=0 and upper=1. Configure requires
 * lower<upper and does not re-evaluate the latched state. Update at/above
 * upper activates, at/below lower deactivates, and otherwise holds. Reset(bool)
 * sets the latch explicitly. Examples: Control/Mapping and Control/Hysteresis.
 */
/**
 * @defgroup DspCore_Control_PID PID
 * @ingroup DspCore_Control
 * @brief Position-form PID with explicit output limits and anti-windup.
 *
 * PidParameters defaults: kp=1, ki=kd=0, outputMin=-1, outputMax=1,
 * derivativeTimeConstant=0. Gains and filter time constant are nonnegative
 * finite values. Limits are finite and strictly ascending. This implements
 * positive-gain negative feedback; actuator polarity belongs to the caller.
 *
 * Each update uses error=setpoint-measurement, P=kp*error,
 * tentative I=clamp(I+ki*error*dt,outputMin,outputMax), and
 * D=-kd*filteredMeasurementRate. Units: kp=output/error,
 * ki=output/(error*second), kd=output*second/measurement. The derivative rate
 * is (measurement-previousMeasurement)/dt. Its optional one-pole filter uses
 * the configured time constant. Setpoint changes alone cause no derivative
 * kick. The first update after Reset uses zero measurement rate.
 *
 * If P+tentativeI+D is above outputMax while I is increasing, or below
 * outputMin while I is decreasing, the update retains the old I. Integration
 * in the recovery direction remains allowed. Finally clamp P+acceptedI+D
 * to the configured output range. This is conditional integration plus an
 * integral-term clamp, not back-calculation or a separate integrator limit.
 *
 * Reset(integral=0) clamps the requested integral, sets Output to that value,
 * clears derivative history, and marks the next measurement as the first.
 * Configure validates transactionally, then clamps stored Integral/Output to
 * new limits while retaining measurement/derivative history. It does not
 * promise bumpless gain changes. No coefficient tuning or automatic reset is
 * performed. Sample/interval/arithmetic failures preserve all state and output.
 * Example: Control/PID demonstrates saturation recovery and a simple plant.
 */
/** @namespace DspCore::Control
 * @brief Signal/system control primitives, independent of MIDI and scheduling.
 */
